                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Figure 6.1 (a) Structure of neuron, (b) Neuromuscular junction"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.499,
            0.722,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "similar synapse finally allows delivery of such impulses from neurons to other cells, such as muscles cells or gland [Fig. 6.1 (b)].",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.534,
            0.723,
            0.584
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It is thus no surprise that nervous tissue is made up of an organised network of nerve cells or neurons, and is specialised for conducting information via electrical impulses from one part of the body to another.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.585,
            0.723,
            0.655
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Look at Fig. 6.1 (a) and identify the parts of a neuron (i) where information is acquired, (ii) through which information travels as an electrical impulse, and (iii) where this impulse must be converted into a chemical signal for onward transmission.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.674,
            0.294,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 6.1"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.714,
            0.574,
            0.729
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "n "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Put some sugar in your mouth. How does it taste?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.73,
            0.696,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Block your nose by pressing it between your thumb and index finger. Now eat sugar again. Is there any difference in its taste?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.759,
            0.696,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "While eating lunch, block your nose in the same way and notice if you can fully appreciate the taste of the food you are eating.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.818,
            0.724,
            0.884
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Is there a difference in how sugar and food taste if your nose is blocked? If so, why might this be happening? Read and talk about possible explanations for these kinds of differences. Do you come across a similar situation when you have a cold?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
